---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/move-rekordbox-library-new-laptop#howto',
  name: 'Move Your rekordbox Library to a New Laptop Without Losing Cues',
  description: 'The safe way to migrate a rekordbox library to a new computer, cues, grids, playlists intact. And the wrong way that quietly destroys years of prep.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/move-rekordbox-library-new-laptop#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/move-rekordbox-library-new-laptop.png',
  // THE STEPS ARE NOW THE PAGE'S OWN WORDS. Rewritten 2026-08-04.
  //
  // The old three steps were compressed paraphrases and dropped the one
  // detail that actually stops a migration dead, logging into the same
  // Pioneer account on the new machine. Google requires HowTo steps to be
  // visible on the page; these four are the four items under "The right way",
  // word for word, minus markup. A DJ reading the AI answer now gets the
  // account step, which the old markup silently deleted.
  //
  // No totalTime: the page says "30-60 min, mostly waiting", which is a
  // range, and ISO 8601 durations cannot express a range. Better absent than
  // a made-up single number.
  step: [
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Old laptop: back up the library',
      text: 'Old laptop: in rekordbox, File → Library → Backup Library → choose "include music files" → target an external drive with enough space.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'New laptop: restore the library',
      text: 'New laptop: install rekordbox, log into the same Pioneer account, then File → Library → Restore Library from the external drive.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Verify',
      text: 'Verify: open your key playlists, load a few tracks, cues and grids should be exactly where you left them.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Re-export your gig USBs',
      text: 'Re-export your gig USBs fresh from the new machine, both drives, and test one on a player before the next gig.',
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base
  title="Move rekordbox to a New Laptop, Cues Intact | Save My Gig"
  description="The safe way to migrate a rekordbox library to a new computer, cues, grids, playlists intact. And the wrong way that quietly destroys years of prep."
  path="/fix/move-rekordbox-library-new-laptop"
  crumbName="Move your library"
  ogImage="/og/move-rekordbox-library-new-laptop.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line amber">Library_Migration</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">NEW LAPTOP,<br>SAME <span class="accent">LIBRARY</span></h1>

    <div class="panel panel-danger">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>The mistake that destroys years of work:</strong> copying your music folder to the new laptop and re-importing it. Music survives, every cue, loop, grid adjustment and playlist dies. Use the built-in Library Backup instead.</p>
    </div>

        {/* FIRST SECTION OPEN ON ARRIVAL (H5, 2026-08-05). The h1 above says
            "DO THIS, IN THIS ORDER" and this page used to answer it with three
            closed rows: zero instructions on screen for someone who is already
            panicking. The first row is the fastest checks, which is what
            everyone needs first, so it is server-rendered open. The rest stay
            closed, and the exclusive `name` still means opening another closes
            this one. `open` on the element, never JavaScript: it has to be in
            the initial HTML and it has to survive JS being off. */}
        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc" open>
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The right way (30-60 min, mostly waiting)</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>Old laptop:</strong> in rekordbox, <span class="mono" translate="no">File → Library → Backup Library</span> → choose "include music files" → target an external drive with enough space.</li>
      <li><strong>New laptop:</strong> install rekordbox, log into the same Pioneer account, then <span class="mono" translate="no">File → Library → Restore Library</span> from the external drive.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify:</strong> open your key playlists, load a few tracks, cues and grids should be exactly where you left them.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-export your gig USBs</strong> fresh from the new machine, both drives, and test one on a player before the next gig.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Cloud alternative</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p>The Cloud Library Sync feature in rekordbox (with a subscription plan) can migrate cues and playlists through Pioneer's cloud, convenient for ongoing two-computer setups. For a one-time move, the local backup is faster, free, and doesn't depend on upload speeds.</p>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">While you're at it</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>This backup file is also your <strong>disaster insurance</strong>. Make one monthly; keep it off the gig bag.</li>
      <li>Old laptop staying alive? Let ONE machine own the gig drives from now on, two writers corrupt device databases. <a href="/fix/playlists-not-showing-cdj">Here's how that ends →</a></li>
    </ul>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="library_migration" />
  </main>
</Base>
